// src/v4/extdeps/languages/ptx.dag
//
// Scope: CUDA model — direction-agnostic. Modeled at the PTX ISA layer
//        (forced by DECISIONS.md L-3 — PTX is the SIMT *IR*; CUDA-C++ is
//        a Shape A source already covered by general modeling, so it
//        would not be a down-the-stack probe at all): the SIMT execution
//        model (grid / block / thread), the memory hierarchy
//        (register / shared / global / etc.), kernels and barriers.
// Anchor: NVIDIA "Parallel Thread Execution ISA Version 8.5" —
//         https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html
//         (release PDF: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/pdf/ptx_isa_8.5.pdf,
//         CUDA Toolkit 12.5). This is the L-2 versioned fidelity authority
//         the carriers below are pinned to (BarrierScope's `.cluster`,
//         ThreadCoordSource's cluster hierarchy, and StateSpace's eight
//         memory regions are derived from this release; a future ISA
//         release modifying any of them is a visible C1 edit). The
//         "CUDA C++ Programming Guide" documents the SIMT execution
//         model the spec encodes.
//
// IN-B FINDING (the T-4.14 probe's deliverable, operator-ratified
//   STOP-or-find discipline per §D): SIMT data-parallelism IS modelable
//   as the existing 5 L1 behaviors — NO 6th `Parallel` / `Kernel`
//   behavior is required. The full mapping lives in the modeling notes
//   section below; the headline:
//     kernel        → Transform (a function over (thread_coord, params))
//     thread grid   → Loop (bounded recursion over the grid's bounded
//                     thread-index cardinality; THESIS:18 "parallelism
//                     is the default" — independence is read from the
//                     dependency graph, scheduling is mechanical)
//     barrier       → Bind (sequencing synchronization point)
//     state space   → effect-typed parameter (a structural Conj field
//                     on the value, per THESIS:401 IN-B "intrinsic to
//                     the type signature, not an annotation layer")
//     @p predicate  → Branch (predicated execution = guarded Branch on
//                     a `.pred` carrier)
//   The 5 behaviors hold; the probe surfaces no substrate gap (no STOP).
//
// Owns:
//   - PTX coordinate classifiers as closed structural carriers:
//     StateSpace (memory hierarchy — with ParamScope / SharedScope
//     sub-qualifiers structurally enforced on `.param` / `.shared`
//     per PTX 8.5 §5.1.7-8 / §6 memory-operand grammar),
//     BarrierScope (CTA/cluster/GPU/sys),
//     ThreadAxis (X/Y/Z), ThreadCoordSource (thread / CTA-in-cluster /
//     cluster / CTA-in-grid hierarchy per PTX ISA 8.5),
//     BitsWidth / IntegerWidth / FloatWidth (per-kind admissible-
//     width sub-enums — kind × width admissibility is structurally
//     enforced by RegisterScalar's Disj-with-payloads below, not
//     deferred to a check), RegisterScalarKind
//     (Bits/Unsigned/Signed/Float/Predicate)
//   - PTX value shapes: RegisterScalar (Disj-with-heterogeneous-
//     payloads, kind × admissible-width × admissible-residence
//     STRUCTURALLY enforced — see RegisterScalar declaration);
//     Dim3 / ThreadCoord (Conj records — Dim3 carries the 🟡 scaffold
//     bridge to std/cardinality.dag, same as std/diagnostic.dag's
//     ByteRange); ThreadHierarchyShape (Disj over launch shapes:
//     FlatLaunch | ClusterLaunch)
//   - PTX cost-realization carrier shape: PtxCost (per-instruction +
//     occupancy components — the per-target cost projection U1 reads)
//   - the IN-B mapping (modeling notes): SIMT → 5 behaviors, no 6th
//
// Owned ELSEWHERE (do not duplicate here):
//   - the bidirectional LanguageModel substrate (grammar productions,
//     C5-fidelity disposition tags, ingest∘emit roundtrip carrier) — the
//     BUNDLED work of T-4 across the 5 Shape A source languages, where
//     "the SHAPE is the work" (TASKS.md T-4 authoring contract). When
//     that shape lands, this file's classifiers slot into the PTX
//     `LanguageModel` instance; the classifiers themselves do not
//     prejudge that shape.
//   - PTX integer / float scalar grounding (`.u32` inhabits `Semiring`,
//     `.s32` inhabits `OrderedRing`, `.f32` inhabits `ApproximateField`)
//     — flows from std/integer.dag / std/machine.dag / std/float.dag
//     (T-3 wave A3) when those carriers + their algebra instance-values
//     land. The PTX register-type vocabulary (kind × width) declared
//     here is the SURFACE through which that grounding parameterizes.
//     Pre-T-3-A3 this file declares no algebra instance-value, by P2
//     single-authority: re-declaring an inhabitance whose carrier file
//     is unwritten would foreclose its eventual authority.
//   - SIMT-as-Bind composition's effect-typing carrier — that is the
//     subject of extdeps/coordination.dag (T-4.8); the IN-B finding
//     here demonstrates the mapping fits the 5 behaviors, and
//     coordination.dag (when it lands) owns the effect-typed Bind
//     carrier the kernel-as-Transform composes through.
//
// Consumes:
//   - nothing — this file is closed-enum + Conj-record declarations
//     only. `Int` is kernel-ambient (Dim3 component; the per-kind
//     width sub-enums BitsWidth/IntegerWidth/FloatWidth are closed
//     enums, not Int-carrying). No algebra instance-values are declared at this
//     phase (see "Owned ELSEWHERE" — PTX scalar inhabitance is
//     downstream of T-3 wave A3 + T-4's LanguageModel shape).
//   (The scaffold header's prior `std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag,
//   std/primitive.dag` Consumes line predated PR #3152's
//   std/primitive.dag decomposition AND the operator-ratified §0
//   scope-narrowing of this probe to "closed classifiers + IN-B
//   finding"; the revised Consumes line above reflects what the file
//   actually imports, not the scaffold's anticipatory list. Header
//   Consumes change pending operator ratification of the systemic
//   scaffold-Consumes reconciliation (T-4 mgr synthesis
//   msg_1fddb75c) — the surface+trigger discipline lets the
//   operator ratify all per-file Consumes strikes uniformly in one
//   pass; this is not worker-settled.)
//
// Status: T-4.14 modeled 2026-05-16 (gentle-deer-446); probe finding:
// NO 6th behavior surfaced. The I/O contract in this header is
// immutable for downstream workers. Splitting this file requires
// explicit operator ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.languages.ptx


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE IN-B FALSIFICATION PROBE (T-4.14, B2-OMNI + IN-B, DECISIONS.md
// L-3). The brief's §D framing is binding: "the STOP discipline is the
// deliverable — if the substrate (5 behaviors / 6 connectives / fail-
// closed) genuinely can't model the target, that is a substrate
// finding to surface, not force." For T-4.14: "STOP if tempted toward
// a 6th behavior." This section records the structural fit and
// names the operator-tier triggers that would have flipped the
// finding to STOP — neither fired.
//
// PROBE OUTCOME — the 5 behaviors hold; the SIMT model fits without a
// 6th. The mapping, member-by-member:
//
//   - KERNEL ↦ Transform.
//     A PTX `.entry` is a function `(thread_coord, kernel_params) ->
//     side-effects-on-state-space`. That is exactly the Transform
//     behavior (function application — INVARIANTS:72 typed-total-λ
//     fragment, node.dag:209 "Transform=application"). The kernel's
//     body is a sub-DAG of the 5 behaviors, identical to a CPU
//     function's body — no kernel-specific behavior surfaces.
//     "Run once per thread" is the SCHEDULING of the surrounding Loop
//     (see next), not a property of the kernel function itself; the
//     Transform IS the kernel callable.
//
//   - THREAD GRID ↦ Loop.
//     The execution-of-a-kernel-across-its-grid is bounded recursion
//     over the grid's bounded thread-index cardinality (gridDim ×
//     blockDim, both Cardinality-bounded). This is exactly the A2
//     `Loop` discipline (node.dag:42-68: bounded recursion, total-by-
//     construction). Per THESIS:18 / node.dag:209 "Parallelism is the
//     default because independence is visible in the structure" —
//     iterations of the kernel-Loop over distinct thread-coords are
//     INDEPENDENT unless they communicate (through `.shared` /
//     `.global` reads/writes or barriers, both visible structural
//     edges). The SIMT schedule (lock-step warp execution) is the
//     mechanical realization of the dependency graph; it is NOT a
//     separate behavior. Tempting alternative: a `Parallel` /
//     `Kernel` behavior carrying "this Loop is data-parallel". That
//     temptation would have been the STOP trigger — but it duplicates
//     what the dependency graph already says (THESIS:18 again:
//     "sequential execution is what needs justification"). Adding
//     such a behavior would be a feedback_no_annotations violation
//     (annotating a property structurally already visible). REJECTED;
//     no STOP needed.
//
//   - BARRIER ↦ Bind.
//     `bar.sync` / `membar` join concurrent threads at a
//     synchronization point. That is exactly the Bind behavior
//     (node.dag:209 "Bind=let" — sequencing). A barrier's SCOPE (CTA
//     / GPU / system) is a Conj-record field on the Bind (the
//     `BarrierScope` classifier below), not a new behavior; same
//     pattern as `bar.sync 0` vs `membar.gl` differing only in scope.
//     Tempting alternative: a `Synchronize` behavior. REJECTED — Bind
//     is the existing sequencing primitive; "synchronize across
//     concurrent threads" is a property of the surrounding parallel
//     Loop reading the Bind, not of the Bind itself.
//
//   - STATE SPACE ↦ effect-typed parameter (structural variant
//     payload). A PTX value's `.reg` / `.shared::cta` /
//     `.shared::cluster` / `.global` / `.const` / `.local` /
//     `.param::entry` / `.param::func` residence is a structural
//     payload on `RegisterScalar`'s typed variants — specifically
//     the `state_space: RegisterStateSpace` field on
//     BitsScalar / UnsignedScalar / SignedScalar / FloatScalar
//     (RegisterStateSpace is the 7-variant typed-scalar-resident
//     sub-classifier of StateSpace, excluding `.tex` since the
//     `.tex` state space holds opaque texture/sampler descriptors
//     per PTX 8.5 §5.1.10, not typed scalars). PredicateScalar is
//     a BARE variant with NO state_space field — per PTX 8.5
//     §5.1.1 predicates are virtual predicate registers (`.reg
//     .pred`) with no memory load/store path; residence is
//     implicit `.reg`. Memory ops (`ld.global`, `st.shared::cta`)
//     are Transforms whose input/output state-space tags constrain
//     valid composition. This IS the THESIS:401 IN-B claim made
//     concrete: effects are "intrinsic to the type signature, NOT
//     an annotation layer." Tempting alternative: a
//     `StateSpaceTransfer` behavior wrapping every ld/st. REJECTED
//     — it would parallel-represent what the Transform's
//     signature already says; the effect-typed parameter is the
//     unannotated primitive.
//
//   - @p PREDICATE ↦ Branch.
//     PTX predicated execution (`@p mov.b32 r1, r2;`) is a guarded
//     Branch on the `.pred` carrier: `if p then op else skip`. The
//     `Branch` behavior already covers this (node.dag:209
//     "Branch=sums"). Predicate negation (`@!p`) is the
//     `BooleanAlgebra<.pred>.complement` instance-value — to land
//     downstream when std/logic.dag's inhabitance form lands;
//     declaring it here would parallel-represent it pre-substrate
//     (P2). For T-4.14 the structural fit is the deliverable; the
//     instance-value follows.
//
// CONCLUSION. The probe's IN-B half closes with no STOP. The B2-OMNI
// half (PTX grammar as declarative bidirectional data) hinges on
// T-4's LanguageModel shape — when that bundled shape lands, this
// file's coordinate classifiers slot into the PTX `LanguageModel`
// instance without revision (their I/O contract is the immutable
// header above).
//
// SCOPE NEGATIVE (operator-tier, do not work around): warp-level
// intrinsics (`%warpid`, `%laneid`, `%smid`), texture / surface
// references, async copies (`cp.async`), tensor cores (`wmma`,
// `mma.sync`), and the `bf16` / vector-pack types (`.f16x2`,
// `.f32x2`) are deliberately not modeled at T-4.14. They sit either
// (a) inside the bundled LanguageModel shape (grammar productions —
// T-4's work), (b) inside the cost-realization detail per release
// pin (PtxCost note below), or (c) inside the SIMT-effect-typed Bind
// substrate that coordination.dag (T-4.8) owns. The classifiers
// declared here are the irreducible SIMT coordinate set; the rest
// composes over them when their substrate lands.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj carrier below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 + all five patterns (Fact-placement /
// Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form / Dimensional / Parameterized-
// family-Practice-7) per modeling-discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified
// binding 2026-05-15, gate (b) — #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the
// proof case; do not omit Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger
// (only coproducts dissolve); scaffold-residual Conj fields (Dim3
// raw Int offsets) carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring std/diagnostic.dag's
// `Extent.ByteRange` precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ParamScope — the closed PTX `.param` state-space sub-qualifier.
// PTX ISA 8.5 §5.1.7 / §6 distinguishes two `.param` sub-spaces in
// the memory-operand grammar (`ld.param::entry` vs `ld.param::func`):
//
//   - ParamEntry — `.param::entry`  kernel-entry parameter memory
//                                   (the parameter buffer at the
//                                   kernel-launch boundary; staged
//                                   by the host launch site, read-
//                                   only inside the kernel)
//   - ParamFunc  — `.param::func`   function-call parameter memory
//                                   (parameters at PTX device-function
//                                   call sites, distinct from the
//                                   kernel-entry parameter buffer)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third `.param` sub-qualifier is an edit
// to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#parameter-state-space
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `.param` operand consumer reads
//      the scope (an `ld.param::entry` is at the kernel boundary; an
//      `ld.param::func` is at a function call site — distinct
//      emission paths); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses. Same
//      argument as StateSpace's nullary-tag form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two are the PTX-
//      spec param-sub-qualifier partition; no richer std/ structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a `.param` operand has EXACTLY ONE
//      sub-qualifier; `.param::entry::func` is not a PTX construct.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are structurally
//      distinct parameter-boundary roles (kernel-entry boundary vs
//      function-call boundary) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way `.param` sub-qualifier partition.
type ParamScope
  = ParamEntry
  | ParamFunc


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SharedScope — the closed PTX `.shared` state-space sub-qualifier.
// PTX ISA 8.5 §5.1.8 / §6 distinguishes two `.shared` sub-spaces in
// the memory-operand grammar (`ld.shared::cta` vs `ld.shared::cluster`,
// the latter being distributed shared memory across cluster CTAs;
// PTX 7.8+ / Hopper / CUDA 9.0):
//
//   - SharedCta     — `.shared::cta`     CTA-scoped shared memory
//                                        (the legacy per-CTA shared
//                                        region; the default when
//                                        sub-qualifier is omitted in
//                                        pre-cluster code)
//   - SharedCluster — `.shared::cluster` cluster-scoped distributed
//                                        shared memory (DSMEM —
//                                        addressable across the
//                                        CTAs of a cluster; PTX 7.8+,
//                                        Hopper / CUDA 9.0 — the
//                                        same cluster surface
//                                        BarrierScope.Cluster and
//                                        ThreadHierarchyShape's
//                                        ClusterLaunch variant
//                                        compose with)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third `.shared` sub-qualifier is an
// edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#shared-state-space
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `.shared` operand consumer
//      reads the scope (`ld.shared::cta` is intra-CTA; `ld.shared
//      ::cluster` is cluster-wide — distinct emission paths and
//      distinct memory-consistency rules per the pinned spec);
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ cluster_wide: Bool }` proxy
//      replaces a closed 2-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Bool (the same M4 smell as RustIntKind's signed-vs-unsigned).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two are the PTX-
//      spec shared-sub-qualifier partition; no richer std/
//      structure. The containment SharedCta ⊑ SharedCluster (a
//      cluster-wide address is reachable from any CTA in the
//      cluster) is derived FROM the closed enum, not a richer
//      source (same parallel-rep smell as BarrierScope's
//      Cta ⊑ Cluster ⊑ Gpu ⊑ System lattice).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a `.shared` operand has EXACTLY ONE
//      sub-qualifier; `.shared::cta::cluster` is not a PTX
//      construct.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are structurally
//      distinct shared-scope partitions (intra-CTA vs cluster-wide
//      distributed) — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over a
//      separately-declared source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way `.shared` sub-qualifier partition.
type SharedScope
  = SharedCta
  | SharedCluster


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// StateSpace — the closed PTX memory-hierarchy classifier. PTX ISA
// 8.5 §5.1 admits eight top-level state spaces; `.param` and
// `.shared` carry sub-qualifiers in the memory-operand grammar
// (§5.1.7 / §5.1.8 / §6 — `.param::{entry,func}` and
// `.shared::{cta,cluster}`), making this enum a Disj-with-mixed-
// arity-variants rather than a flat 8-way enum: nullary tags for
// state spaces with no sub-qualifier, named-field payloads for
// `Param` and `Shared` to carry their sub-qualifiers structurally.
// Illegal "Shared without scope" or "Param without entry/func
// distinction" are unrepresentable (the INVARIANTS P2 illegal-
// states-unrepresentable shape; the same Disj-with-payloads design
// rust.dag's RustScalar took for kind×width admissibility and
// ptx.dag's RegisterScalar took for kind × admissible-width).
//
// (Refactored 2026-05-16 per operator BLOCKING review on PR #3170:
// the prior flat 8-way enum collapsed PTX 8.5 sub-qualifier facts
// — `.param::entry` vs `.param::func`, `.shared::cta` vs
// `.shared::cluster` — into the bare Param/Shared tags. INVARIANTS
// P2 spec-fidelity violated: facts the spec's memory-operand
// grammar distinguishes were dropped before consumers could
// enforce them.)
//
//   - Reg       — `.reg`     virtual register (per-thread; the
//                            register-allocated value's home)
//   - SReg      — `.sreg`    special register (read-only thread-
//                            coord and warp/lane intrinsics; per-
//                            thread)
//   - Const     — `.const`   constant memory (read-only; per-grid)
//   - Global    — `.global`  global memory (read-write; per-grid)
//   - Local     — `.local`   local memory (per-thread spill region)
//   - Param     — `.param::{entry,func}` parameter memory,
//                            sub-qualified by ParamScope (the
//                            kernel-entry boundary vs the function-
//                            call boundary)
//   - Shared    — `.shared::{cta,cluster}` shared memory,
//                            sub-qualified by SharedScope (CTA-
//                            scoped vs cluster-distributed)
//   - Tex       — `.tex`     texture memory (read-only; per-grid)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A ninth state space is an edit to THIS
// declaration — a visible diff routed to operator ratification. The
// PTX spec adds state spaces across releases (e.g. recent additions
// for cooperative-group memory); the pin discipline (L-2 versioned
// fidelity authority) means each release-version model EDITS this
// declaration explicitly, never silently extends it.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#state-spaces
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every memory-op Transform branches on
//      the operand's StateSpace (`ld.global` vs `ld.shared::cta`
//      vs `ld.shared::cluster` are distinct ops with distinct
//      emission syntax and memory-consistency rules); shared
//      classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the variants to a
//      uniform record `{ kind: StateSpaceKind, param_scope:
//      Option<…>, shared_scope: Option<…> }` reintroduces the
//      illegal states "Param with shared_scope set" and "Global
//      with param_scope set" the Disj-with-payloads exists to
//      forbid — same argument as RegisterScalar's variant-is-data
//      ledger entry. The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: PTX state spaces are
//      the PTX spec's primitive memory-region taxonomy; they do
//      not trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure (no Lattice
//      / no Field on the eight tags — read-only vs read-write is
//      a separate per-state-space property, not a partial order
//      over them).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a PTX value lives in EXACTLY ONE state
//      space; the directive `.global .shared` is a syntax error in
//      PTX itself. The variants' INTERNAL structure differs (the
//      sub-qualified variants carry a scope payload; the others
//      don't) — they cannot be coordinates of a single record
//      without the illegal-state smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the eight have structurally
//      distinct semantic roles (per-thread vs per-CTA vs per-grid;
//      readable vs read-only; addressable vs intrinsic-only; with
//      vs without sub-qualifiers) — NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a source set (Practice 7). The variants
//      carry structurally-distinct payload shapes (Reg / SReg /
//      Const / Global / Local / Tex are bare; Param carries
//      ParamScope; Shared carries SharedScope) — not projection-
//      uniform.
// Terminal: the closed eight-way PTX memory-hierarchy classifier
// with structural sub-qualifier enforcement on `.param` and
// `.shared`; a 9th state space is the C1 stop-signal (a visible
// ratified edit to this declaration, STRUCTURE.md commitment #1).
type StateSpace
  = Reg
  | SReg
  | Const
  | Global
  | Local
  | Param { scope: ParamScope }
  | Shared { scope: SharedScope }
  | Tex


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BarrierScope — the closed PTX synchronization scope classifier. The
// scope at which a Bind-as-barrier synchronizes (`bar.sync` / `membar`
// / `fence` directives carry exactly one of four scopes per the
// pinned PTX ISA 8.5 §8.5 memory-consistency model).
//
//   - Cta      — `.cta`      within-CTA (within-block) synchronization
//                            (`bar.sync 0;` is `.cta`-scoped by default;
//                            `membar.cta` / `fence.acq_rel.cta`)
//   - Cluster  — `.cluster`  thread-block-cluster scope (PTX 7.8+,
//                            Hopper / CUDA 9.0 thread block clusters;
//                            `membar.cluster` / `fence.*.cluster` — a
//                            cluster groups multiple CTAs that share
//                            distributed shared memory)
//   - Gpu      — `.gpu` (alias `.gl`)
//                            device-wide (within-GPU) memory ordering
//                            (`membar.gl` / `fence.*.gpu`)
//   - System   — `.sys`      system-wide (across host/device) ordering
//                            (`membar.sys` / `fence.*.sys`)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fifth scope is an edit to THIS
// declaration routed to operator ratification under the L-2 pin
// discipline (the pinned PTX ISA version is the fidelity authority
// — a future ISA release introducing a fifth scope is a visible diff
// to this enum, never a silent extension).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Bind-as-barrier reads the scope
//      uniformly (the synchronization radius is the same datum every
//      consumer needs); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: nullary tags from a closed PTX-defined
//      set. A `{ radius: Int }` proxy (1=cta, 2=cluster, 3=gpu,
//      4=sys) replaces a closed 4-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int (radius=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4
//      (closed sets are enums, not numeric proxies). Same shape as
//      algebra.dag's `Ordering` ledger.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the four scopes are the
//      PTX memory-fence primitive radii; they do not trace to a
//      richer std/ algebraic structure (a `BoundedLattice<Scope>`
//      with Cta ⊑ Cluster ⊑ Gpu ⊑ System would be a parallel
//      representation of "stronger fence subsumes weaker" — a derived
//      property of the tags, not a richer source).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single barrier directive carries
//      EXACTLY ONE scope; `membar.cta.gl` is not a PTX construct.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four are distinct
//      synchronization-radius primitives — NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a source set (Practice 7). The four radii are
//      the PTX spec's irreducible scope partition for the pinned ISA
//      version.
// Terminal: the closed four-way synchronization scope for the pinned
// PTX ISA 8.5 §8.5 spec.
type BarrierScope
  = Cta
  | Cluster
  | Gpu
  | System


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadAxis — the closed PTX coordinate axis classifier. PTX exposes
// thread / block indices as 3-vectors (`%tid.x/.y/.z`, `%ctaid.x/.y/.z`);
// ThreadAxis is the axis SELECTOR for one of those components.
//
//   - X — the x-axis component (the SIMT-fast axis: lanes within a
//         warp vary along x first; the layout convention threadIdx.x
//         carries)
//   - Y — the y-axis component
//   - Z — the z-axis component
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). PTX has fixed 3-D thread/block addressing
// across all current versions; a fourth axis would be a substantive
// ISA change routed to operator ratification.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ThreadCoord consumer that reads
//      a specific axis (e.g. emission of `%tid.x`) branches on the
//      axis tag; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ component: Int }` proxy (0/1/2)
//      replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int (component=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4 the same
//      way `Ordering` does (algebra.dag:332). The enum IS the
//      faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: X/Y/Z are the basis
//      labels of a 3-D coordinate vector; they do not trace to a
//      richer std/ structure (no algebra over the labels themselves;
//      the algebra is on the bounded-Int dim values, separately).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a `.x` / `.y` / `.z` axis selector picks
//      EXACTLY ONE axis; mutually-exclusive, not simultaneous
//      coordinates. (`%tid` as a whole vector is the Dim3 record,
//      separate from the per-axis selector.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: X/Y/Z are positional labels of
//      a fixed 3-D vector — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over
//      a source set (Practice 7). They are the closed three-axis
//      basis, not a copy of any enumerable set.
// Terminal: the closed three-way axis selector of PTX's 3-D
// coordinate addressing.
type ThreadAxis
  = X
  | Y
  | Z


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadCoordSource — the closed PTX thread-coordinate hierarchy
// classifier. PTX ISA 8.5 §5.4 / §11 exposes FOUR structural levels of
// thread coordinate (three legacy + one cluster intrinsic family that
// landed in PTX 7.8 for Hopper / CUDA 9.0 thread block clusters and
// is present in the pinned 8.5 spec). Warp / lane / SM intrinsics
// (`%warpid`, `%laneid`, `%smid`) are scope-negative for T-4.14 (see
// header SCOPE NEGATIVE) — they are realizations of the SIMT schedule,
// not a coordinate axis the model admits as substrate.
//
//   - ThreadInCta    — `%tid.{x,y,z}`           thread position within
//                                               the CTA (block)
//   - CtaInCluster   — `%cluster_ctaid.{x,y,z}` CTA position within the
//                                               cluster (PTX 7.8+,
//                                               Hopper / CUDA 9.0)
//   - ClusterInGrid  — `%clusterid.{x,y,z}`     cluster position within
//                                               the grid (PTX 7.8+)
//   - CtaInGrid      — `%ctaid.{x,y,z}`         CTA position within the
//                                               grid (the cluster-
//                                               independent flat
//                                               coordinate; remains a
//                                               first-class intrinsic
//                                               in PTX 8.5 alongside
//                                               the cluster reads)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fifth hierarchy level is an edit to THIS
// declaration routed to operator ratification under the L-2 pin
// discipline (pinned PTX ISA 8.5).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ThreadCoord consumer reads the
//      source uniformly to know which intrinsic register family it
//      emits; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ level: Int }` (0=thread,
//      1=cta-in-cluster, 2=cluster, 3=cta-in-grid) replaces a closed
//      4-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded Int — violates
//      P2 + M4 (algebra.dag:332 `Ordering` precedent).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the four hierarchy
//      levels are the PTX coordinate-space partition; they do not
//      trace to a richer std/ structure (one could attempt a
//      `PartialOrder<ThreadCoordSource>` containment along Thread ⊑
//      Cta ⊑ Cluster ⊑ Grid, but that would be a parallel
//      representation of what is already a structural containment
//      — feedback_no_engine parallel-rep smell).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single coordinate read picks EXACTLY
//      ONE intrinsic family; mutually-exclusive alternatives, not
//      simultaneous coordinates. (A kernel that uses several
//      addresses each through a separate read; it does not co-locate
//      them.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four are distinct
//      coordinate-space partitions — NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a source set (Practice 7). They are the PTX
//      8.5 spec's irreducible coordinate-intrinsic partition for
//      the cluster-aware hierarchy.
// Terminal: the closed four-way thread-coordinate hierarchy at the
// SIMT-substrate layer the probe models, pinned to PTX ISA 8.5.
type ThreadCoordSource
  = ThreadInCta
  | CtaInCluster
  | ClusterInGrid
  | CtaInGrid


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BitsWidth — the closed PTX untyped-bits-vector width classifier.
// The PTX ISA 8.5 §5.2 fundamental-types section admits exactly five
// untyped `.b{n}` widths (a `.b1` form does NOT exist; the 1-bit
// register is `.pred` only).
//
//   - Bits8    — `.b8`
//   - Bits16   — `.b16`
//   - Bits32   — `.b32`
//   - Bits64   — `.b64`
//   - Bits128  — `.b128`    (untyped 128-bit value used by `mov.b128`
//                            and vector load/store instructions)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A `.b256` / `.b512` is an edit to THIS
// declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#fundamental-types
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `BitsScalar` consumer reads the
//      width to size the value; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (8/16/32/
//      64/128) replaces a closed 5-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int (bits=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4.
//   3. Algebraic form — partially N/A: the bit-counts form an
//      ordered set, but the ORDER is derived FROM the closed enum
//      (algebra.dag U1 engine-debt smell — same argument as
//      rust.dag's RustIntWidth).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed bits-vector has EXACTLY
//      ONE width; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five widths are the PTX-
//      admitted bit-counts; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a separately-declared source set (Practice
//      7).
// Terminal: the closed five-way PTX untyped-bits-width classifier.
type BitsWidth
  = Bits8
  | Bits16
  | Bits32
  | Bits64
  | Bits128


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// IntegerWidth — the closed PTX typed-integer (signed / unsigned)
// width classifier. The PTX ISA 8.5 §5.2 fundamental-types section
// admits exactly four typed-integer widths: `.u8/.u16/.u32/.u64` and
// `.s8/.s16/.s32/.s64`. A `.u128` / `.s128` form does NOT exist (the
// 128-bit width is `.b128`-only — untyped); a `.u1` / `.s1` form does
// NOT exist (the 1-bit register is `.pred` only).
//
//   - Bits8   — `.u8`  / `.s8`
//   - Bits16  — `.u16` / `.s16`
//   - Bits32  — `.u32` / `.s32`
//   - Bits64  — `.u64` / `.s64`
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A `.u128` / `.s128` (currently NOT in PTX
// 8.5) is an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#fundamental-types
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `UnsignedScalar` / `SignedScalar`
//      consumer reads the width to size the integer; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (8/16/32/64)
//      replaces a closed 4-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int (bits=42 representable; bits=1 / bits=128 would also be
//      admitted, but PTX 8.5 doesn't define `.u1` / `.s1` / `.u128`
//      / `.s128`) — violates P2 + M4.
//   3. Algebraic form — partially N/A: the bit-counts form an
//      ordered set, but the ORDER is derived FROM the closed enum
//      (same argument as BitsWidth).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed integer has EXACTLY ONE
//      width.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four widths are the PTX-
//      admitted typed-integer bit-counts; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). One could attempt a projection from BitsWidth's
//      five-element set by removing Bits128, but the partition is
//      defined by the PTX spec's choice of which widths to admit
//      for typed-integer interpretation — that choice IS the
//      discriminant, not a mechanical projection.
// Terminal: the closed four-way PTX typed-integer-width classifier.
type IntegerWidth
  = Bits8
  | Bits16
  | Bits32
  | Bits64


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FloatWidth — the closed PTX typed-float width classifier. The PTX
// ISA 8.5 §5.2 fundamental-types section admits exactly three typed-
// float widths: `.f16` (IEEE-754 binary16 half-precision), `.f32`
// (IEEE-754 binary32 single-precision), and `.f64` (IEEE-754 binary64
// double-precision). `.bf16` (brain-float-16) is a SEPARATE kind in
// PTX 8.5 (not a width of `.f`); it is scope-negative for this slice
// pending the bf16-kind declaration (see header SCOPE NEGATIVE).
// `.f8` / `.f128` do NOT exist in PTX 8.5.
//
//   - Bits16  — `.f16`    (IEEE-754 binary16 half-precision)
//   - Bits32  — `.f32`    (IEEE-754 binary32 single-precision)
//   - Bits64  — `.f64`    (IEEE-754 binary64 double-precision)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). An `.f128` / `.f256` (currently NOT in
// PTX 8.5) is an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin
// discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#fundamental-types
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every `FloatScalar` consumer reads
//      the width to size the float; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (16/32/64)
//      replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int — violates P2 + M4.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the three widths
//      structurally inhabit the SAME algebra (ApproximateField per
//      std/float.dag scaffold's anchor); they do not partition into
//      distinct algebraic structures (.f16 / .f32 / .f64 are all
//      ApproximateFields, only the precision differs).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed float has EXACTLY ONE
//      width.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the three widths are the PTX-
//      admitted typed-float bit-counts (the IEEE-754 binary-format
//      subset PTX 8.5 stabilizes); they are NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a separately-declared source set (Practice
//      7). The richer IEEE-754 source (binary16/32/64/128) is the
//      spec's universe, but PTX admits the binary16/32/64 subset
//      typed — the choice of "which widths PTX stabilizes as `.f`"
//      is the partition discriminant.
// Terminal: the closed three-way PTX typed-float-width classifier.
type FloatWidth
  = Bits16
  | Bits32
  | Bits64


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterScalarKind — the closed PTX scalar-type kind classifier.
// PTX scalar types partition into exactly five kinds per the PTX ISA
// 8.5 §5.2 fundamental-types section:
//
//   - Bits      — `.b{w}`   untyped bit-vector (width-only, no
//                           arithmetic semantics; admissible widths
//                           BitsWidth = {8/16/32/64/128})
//   - Unsigned  — `.u{w}`   unsigned modular integer (Semiring;
//                           admissible widths IntegerWidth =
//                           {8/16/32/64})
//   - Signed    — `.s{w}`   signed two's-complement integer
//                           (OrderedRing; admissible widths
//                           IntegerWidth = {8/16/32/64})
//   - Float     — `.f{w}`   IEEE-754 floating-point (ApproximateField;
//                           admissible widths FloatWidth =
//                           {16/32/64})
//   - Predicate — `.pred`   1-bit boolean (BooleanAlgebra; width is
//                           always 1, no width payload — the kind
//                           alone determines the value's shape)
//
// THIS ENUM IS A META-CLASSIFIER (Owns the K-dimension partition only,
// not the cross-product); the kind × admissible-width admissibility
// is STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED by the `RegisterScalar` Disj-with-payloads
// declaration below — each Disj variant carries EXACTLY its kind-
// admissible width sub-enum (or no width payload, for `Predicate`),
// so illegal kind × width pairs like `Predicate + Bits64` or `Float +
// Bits8` are unrepresentable (the INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-
// unrepresentable shape; the same design choice rust.dag #3174 took
// with RustScalar's Disj-with-payloads, and the operator review on
// PR #3170 that drove this refactor: the prior Conj-record
// `RegisterScalar { kind, width, state_space }` made illegal combos
// CONSTRUCTIBLE pending a "future check" — exactly the v3 fail-open
// failure mode INVARIANTS P2 + Practice 2/6 + the
// `feedback_dissolve_then_check` discipline forbid).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A sixth kind (e.g. brain-float `bf` as a
// kind distinct from Float — PTX 8.5 does have `.bf16` as a separate
// type, scope-negative for this slice; see header SCOPE NEGATIVE) is
// an edit to THIS declaration under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#fundamental-types
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every RegisterScalar consumer reads
//      the kind via the Disj-variant tag to dispatch (which algebra
//      instance applies; which width sub-enum is admissible); shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "u32" => … }` smell). The enum IS the faithful
//      form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a std/algebra
//      structure (Unsigned→Semiring, Signed→OrderedRing,
//      Float→ApproximateField, Predicate→BooleanAlgebra, Bits→no
//      arithmetic algebra by design). That grounding is an
//      INHABITANCE relation — owned by the inhabiting carrier file
//      (std/integer.dag for Unsigned/Signed, std/float.dag for
//      Float, std/logic.dag for Predicate) per P2. The kind enum
//      itself stays a closed classifier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single scalar register has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Kind × width is
//      the Cartesian product on `RegisterScalar`, but the cross-
//      product is HETEROGENEOUS by kind — each kind admits a
//      DIFFERENT width sub-enum, so the product is NOT a flat
//      grid; it is the Disj-with-payloads below.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five kinds are the PTX-
//      spec kind partition; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a separately-declared source set (Practice
//      7). One could attempt `Kind<algebra: AlgebraStructure>`
//      parameterized over an algebra-structure set, but only four
//      of the five ground in algebra (Bits has no arithmetic
//      algebra by design); the partition does not project from a
//      single source set.
// Terminal: the closed five-way PTX scalar-kind classifier; this
// enum is the meta-tag the RegisterScalar Disj's variants are
// projections of, not a separate axis.
type RegisterScalarKind
  = Bits
  | Unsigned
  | Signed
  | Float
  | Predicate


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterStateSpace — the closed PTX typed-scalar-resident state-
// space sub-classifier. PTX ISA 8.5 §5.1 lists 8 state spaces; the
// `.tex` state space (§5.1.10) is for TEXTURE/SAMPLER/SURFACE
// DESCRIPTORS (the opaque types `texref` / `sampref` / `surfref`),
// NOT for typed scalar storage — a typed scalar value cannot reside
// in `.tex`. So the typed-scalar-residence admissibility set is the
// 7-variant subset of StateSpace excluding `Tex`.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). If a future PTX revision adds a typed-
// scalar-resident state space, both this enum AND StateSpace get
// coordinated edits.
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#state-spaces
//       (Reference §5.1; the `.tex` exclusion is grounded in §5.1.10
//       texture, sampler, and surface descriptor variables — opaque
//       types, not typed-scalar storage).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every RegisterScalar consumer reads
//      the residence to dispatch to the right memory-operation
//      family (`ld.global` / `ld.shared::cta` / `ld.const` etc.);
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing to `StateSpace`
//      reintroduces the `.tex`-residence illegal state this enum
//      exists to forbid (a typed scalar cannot live in `.tex`); the
//      sub-classifier IS the dissolved form (same admissibility-
//      sub-enum pattern as BitsWidth / IntegerWidth / FloatWidth
//      relative to a hypothetical AllPtxWidths meta-enum).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: these are the spec's
//      typed-scalar-resident state spaces (StateSpace minus `.tex`);
//      no richer std/ structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed scalar lives in EXACTLY
//      ONE residence; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the seven variants are
//      structurally distinct (per-thread vs per-CTA vs per-grid;
//      readable vs read-only; with vs without sub-qualifiers —
//      Param and Shared carry payloads) — NOT projection-uniform
//      over any richer source. The variants are the PTX-spec
//      typed-scalar-resident partition, restricted from the full
//      StateSpace partition by exclusion of `.tex` (which is for
//      opaque descriptors, not typed scalars).
// Terminal: the closed seven-way PTX typed-scalar-resident state-
// space sub-classifier; a typed scalar in `.tex` is structurally
// unrepresentable (per PTX 8.5 §5.1.10).
type RegisterStateSpace
  = ResReg
  | ResSReg
  | ResConst
  | ResGlobal
  | ResLocal
  | ResParam { scope: ParamScope }
  | ResShared { scope: SharedScope }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterScalar — the typed PTX scalar register carrier: kind ×
// (kind-admissible width) × (kind-admissible residence). Disj-with-
// named-field-variant-payloads so that BOTH kind × width AND kind ×
// residence admissibility are STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED — illegal pairs
// like `Predicate + Bits64`, `Float + Bits8`, `Unsigned + Bits128`,
// `Bits + Bits1`, `Predicate + Global` (or any non-.reg state space
// — `.pred` is reg-only per PTX 8.5 §5.1.1), or any-scalar + `.tex`
// (per §5.1.10 `.tex` is for opaque descriptors not typed scalars)
// are unrepresentable (not "rejected by a check" — UNREPRESENTABLE;
// the INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable shape, same
// design choice rust.dag #3174 took for RustScalar).
//
// Per-variant residence admissibility (refactored 2026-05-16 per
// operator REQUEST_CHANGES on PR #3170, openai-pro/gpt-5.5-pro
// review: the prior `state_space: StateSpace` on every variant
// admitted PTX-invalid combinations — `PredicateScalar { state_space
// : Global }` was constructible despite §5.1.1 saying `.pred` is
// reg-only, and any-scalar-in-`.tex` was constructible despite
// §5.1.10 reserving `.tex` for opaque descriptors):
//   - Bits / Unsigned / Signed / Float scalars carry `state_space:
//     RegisterStateSpace` (the 7-variant `.tex`-excluding sub-
//     classifier above); .tex residence is unrepresentable for
//     typed scalars.
//   - PredicateScalar carries NO state_space field — predicates are
//     virtual predicate registers (`.reg .pred` per §5.1.1) with no
//     memory load/store path; residence is implicit `.reg`, NOT a
//     freely-chosen field. `PredicateScalar { state_space: Global }`
//     was the canonical illegal state this refactor forecloses.
//
//   - BitsScalar      — { width: BitsWidth, state_space:
//                          RegisterStateSpace }
//                       e.g. `.b32` in `.global` is BitsScalar
//                       { width: Bits32, state_space: ResGlobal }
//   - UnsignedScalar  — { width: IntegerWidth, state_space:
//                          RegisterStateSpace }
//                       e.g. `.u32` in `.reg` is UnsignedScalar
//                       { width: Bits32, state_space: ResReg }
//   - SignedScalar    — { width: IntegerWidth, state_space:
//                          RegisterStateSpace }
//                       e.g. `.s64` in `.shared::cta` is
//                       SignedScalar { width: Bits64, state_space:
//                       ResShared { scope: SharedCta } }
//   - FloatScalar     — { width: FloatWidth, state_space:
//                          RegisterStateSpace }
//                       e.g. `.f32` in `.const` is FloatScalar
//                       { width: Bits32, state_space: ResConst }
//   - PredicateScalar — bare variant (no state_space field; implicit
//                       `.reg`). Predicate is intrinsically 1-bit
//                       per PTX 8.5 §5.1.1 and lives only in virtual
//                       predicate registers; no memory residence
//                       is admitted by the spec.
//
// `.bf16` is DELIBERATELY NOT a variant of this Disj: PTX 8.5 §5.2
// treats `.bf16` as a separate kind distinct from `.f`, and modeling
// it requires a sixth `BitsBrain` / `BFloat` kind variant — scope-
// negative for this slice (header SCOPE NEGATIVE; a future C1 edit
// when the bf16 surface lands).
//
// Texture/sampler/surface descriptors (`.tex` opaque types `texref`
// / `sampref` / `surfref` per §5.1.10) are also DELIBERATELY NOT
// modeled as variants of this Disj — they are opaque descriptor
// types, not typed scalars; modeling them needs a separate
// `TextureReference` / `SamplerReference` / `SurfaceReference`
// carrier (scope-negative for this slice; lands when the texture-
// op surface needs modeling).
//
// The state_space FIELD on the typed-scalar variants is the
// structural surface of the IN-B claim "state space is an effect-
// typed parameter, intrinsic to the type signature, NOT an
// annotation layer" (THESIS:401, scaffold header IN-B note).
// Memory-op Transforms read this field directly from operand types;
// there is no separate state-space-annotation layer.
//
// SEAM (where D2 inhabitance plugs in). When the D2 form decision
// lands (operator-pending), the per-variant inhabitance instance-
// values land in this file with these typed variants as their
// carriers — UnsignedScalar { width: Bits32, ... } is the carrier
// for PTX `.u32` inhabitance into Semiring (or its v4-equivalent
// per the D2-ratified form); FloatScalar { width: Bits32, ... } is
// the carrier for `.f32` → ApproximateField; PredicateScalar is
// the carrier for `.pred` → BooleanAlgebra. The seam itself is the
// typed variant; the inhabitance value is the deferred row.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every PTX-typed register consumer
//      (operand typing for instructions, emission of `.b32` /
//      `.u32` / `.f32` / `.pred` syntax, cost-realization, eventual
//      inhabitance dispatch) reads the scalar's kind + kind-
//      admissible width + kind-admissible residence through this
//      carrier; shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the variants to a
//      uniform record `{ kind: RegisterScalarKind, width:
//      Option<…>, state_space: Option<RegisterStateSpace> }`
//      reintroduces exactly the illegal states this Disj-with-
//      payloads design exists to forbid (Predicate-with-state-
//      space, Float-with-Bits8, Unsigned-with-Bits128, etc.). The
//      5-variant Disj IS the dissolved form (P2 + Practice 2/6 +
//      M4).
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each variant grounds in a std/
//      algebra structure (Unsigned → Semiring; Signed →
//      OrderedRing; Float → ApproximateField; Predicate →
//      BooleanAlgebra; Bits → no arithmetic algebra by design).
//      That grounding IS the inhabitance walk owned ELSEWHERE per
//      P2; the Disj structure here is the closed surface, not a
//      collapsing target.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed PTX scalar IS EXACTLY
//      ONE variant of this Disj; mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//      The shapes differ across variants (PredicateScalar has NO
//      payload — bare; the typed variants each carry their kind-
//      admissible width sub-enum + RegisterStateSpace residence) —
//      they cannot be coordinates of a single record without the
//      illegal-state smell.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five variants are the PTX
//      spec's primitive-kind partition; they are NOT one generic
//      `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared source set
//      (Practice 7). The variants correspond to STRUCTURALLY
//      DISTINCT admissible-width sub-enums (BitsWidth /
//      IntegerWidth / FloatWidth / no-width-no-residence for
//      Predicate) and structurally distinct algebraic groundings —
//      they are not projection-uniform over any richer source. The
//      rust.dag #3174 precedent established this Disj-with-
//      heterogeneous-payloads form for exactly this reason.
// Terminal: the closed five-variant PTX scalar-register typed-value
// sum with kind × admissible-width AND kind × admissible-residence
// admissibility STRUCTURALLY enforced; illegal pairs are
// unrepresentable by construction.
type RegisterScalar
  = BitsScalar { width: BitsWidth, state_space: RegisterStateSpace }
  | UnsignedScalar { width: IntegerWidth, state_space: RegisterStateSpace }
  | SignedScalar { width: IntegerWidth, state_space: RegisterStateSpace }
  | FloatScalar { width: FloatWidth, state_space: RegisterStateSpace }
  | PredicateScalar


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Dim3 — the PTX 3-D dimension/index carrier: an x/y/z triple of
// kernel-launch dimensions or coordinate indices. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger — only coproducts dissolve).
//
//   - x, y, z       the three-axis components
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`).
// With only kernel-ambient `Int` available at the T-4.14 phase,
// `{ x: Int, y: Int, z: Int }` STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states
// (negative dims; zero dims; dims exceeding the PTX max per axis —
// 65535 for x of gridDim, 1024 for thread count of blockDim, etc.).
// modeling-discipline.md §2 (illegal-states-unrepresentable) forbids
// admitting them; no bounded-Cardinality refinement substrate exists
// yet to exclude them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the
// three bridge properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established
// 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//       the terminal bounded-axis shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only valid PTX
//       dimensions per the pinned PTX-ISA version's per-axis limits.
//       Until the substrate enforces it the invariant is producer-
//       side; a Dim3 violating it is a producer bug, not a legal
//       value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `x` / `y` / `z`
//       become refined bounded-positive-Int axes (positive and
//       within the PTX-version-pinned per-axis maxima), and this
//       scaffold dissolves.
type Dim3 {
  x: Int
  y: Int
  z: Int
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadHierarchyShape — a PTX kernel launch's structural shape. PTX
// ISA 8.5 §11 admits TWO structurally distinct launch shapes — the
// legacy flat launch (the grid is a flat collection of CTAs) and the
// cluster-enabled launch (the grid is a collection of clusters, each
// a collection of CTAs; PTX 7.8+, Hopper / CUDA 9.0). Each shape
// exposes a structurally DIFFERENT dimensional carrier set; encoding
// the launch shape as a Disj-with-payloads makes the carriers
// admissibility STRUCTURALLY ENFORCED — a flat launch does not carry
// cluster dims, a cluster launch does, and "a flat launch with
// cluster_dim=(1,1,1)" is unrepresentable as a category-mistake.
// (Refactored 2026-05-16 per operator BLOCKING review on PR #3170:
// the prior Conj record `{ grid_dim, block_dim }` had no carriers
// for `%cluster_nctaid` / `%nclusterid` bounds, so cluster
// coordinate-bound facts had no single authority in the model — the
// INVARIANTS P2 single-authority shape violated for the cluster
// half of the hierarchy.)
//
//   - FlatLaunch     — legacy non-cluster launch shape:
//                       grid_dim   number of CTAs along x/y/z in the
//                                  grid (`%nctaid.{x,y,z}` /
//                                  `gridDim.{x,y,z}`)
//                       block_dim  number of threads along x/y/z
//                                  within each CTA (`%ntid.{x,y,z}`
//                                  / `blockDim.{x,y,z}`)
//   - ClusterLaunch  — cluster-enabled launch shape (PTX 7.8+):
//                       cluster_grid_dim   number of clusters along
//                                          x/y/z in the grid
//                                          (`%nclusterid.{x,y,z}`)
//                       cluster_dim        number of CTAs along
//                                          x/y/z within each cluster
//                                          (`%cluster_nctaid.{x,y,z}`)
//                       block_dim          number of threads along
//                                          x/y/z within each CTA
//                                          (`%ntid.{x,y,z}`)
//                       The flat grid-CTA count `%nctaid` remains
//                       readable in cluster launches and equals
//                       `cluster_grid_dim × cluster_dim` (element-
//                       wise); the model RECONSTRUCTS the flat
//                       coordinate from the hierarchical carriers
//                       rather than redundantly carrying it
//                       (P2 single-authority — `%nctaid` is the
//                       cluster-grid product, not an independent
//                       fact).
//
// The product `(grid_dim × block_dim)` for FlatLaunch — or
// `(cluster_grid_dim × cluster_dim × block_dim)` for ClusterLaunch —
// is the BOUNDED CARDINALITY of the thread-grid Loop per the IN-B
// mapping (kernel iteration runs over this finite set — the A2
// boundedness that lets the Loop be total-by-construction).
//
// Spec: https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/parallel-thread-execution/index.html#thread-hierarchy
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every kernel-launch consumer reads
//      the shape to bound the thread-grid Loop's cardinality and
//      to wire the coordinate-intrinsic reads (`%tid` / `%ctaid` /
//      `%cluster_ctaid` / `%clusterid`) to their respective bounds;
//      shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: collapsing the two variants to a
//      single record `{ grid_dim, cluster_dim?, cluster_grid_dim?,
//      block_dim }` reintroduces the illegal state "flat launch
//      with cluster dims set" the Disj-with-payloads exists to
//      forbid, AND admits the redundant state "cluster launch
//      whose explicit grid_dim disagrees with cluster_grid_dim ×
//      cluster_dim" — exactly the P2 single-authority violation
//      this refactor addresses.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two launch shapes
//      are the PTX-spec launch-mode partition; they do not trace
//      to a richer std/ algebraic structure.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single launch IS EXACTLY ONE shape
//      — either flat (no cluster decomposition) OR cluster-enabled
//      (explicit cluster decomposition). PTX launches the kernel
//      with one or the other launch-mode parameter at the host-
//      side launch site; they are mutually-exclusive alternatives.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two variants are NOT one
//      generic `F<X>` / projection over a separately-declared
//      source set (Practice 7). The variants carry structurally
//      distinct dimensional-carrier sets (FlatLaunch has 2 Dim3s;
//      ClusterLaunch has 3) — they are not projection-uniform
//      over any richer source. Same shape as rust.dag's
//      RustScalar Disj-with-heterogeneous-payloads.
// Terminal: the closed two-variant PTX-launch-shape sum; the
// cluster-launch path carries `%cluster_nctaid` and `%nclusterid`
// bounds in single authority (no redundancy with the flat-launch
// path); a third launch shape is the C1 stop-signal (a visible
// ratified edit to this declaration under the L-2 pin discipline).
type ThreadHierarchyShape
  = FlatLaunch { grid_dim: Dim3, block_dim: Dim3 }
  | ClusterLaunch { cluster_grid_dim: Dim3, cluster_dim: Dim3, block_dim: Dim3 }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadCoord — a typed thread-coordinate read: a specific axis
// component of a specific hierarchy level. Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger).
//
//   - source        which hierarchy level (thread-in-CTA or CTA-in-
//                   grid; warp / lane / SM are scope-negative for
//                   T-4.14)
//   - axis          which 3-D axis component (X / Y / Z)
//
// Concretely: `ThreadCoord { source: ThreadInCta, axis: X }` is the
// `%tid.x` intrinsic; `{ source: CtaInGrid, axis: Z }` is `%ctaid.z`.
type ThreadCoord {
  source: ThreadCoordSource
  axis: ThreadAxis
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PtxCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the
// PTX `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing PTX-side
// realization cost — both components per the scaffold header's
// "per-instruction + occupancy cost on a PTX target" claim:
//
//   - instruction_cost   per-instruction throughput cost (the U1
//                        per-target-op cost projection — the `map`
//                        side of `Homomorphism<…, …, PtxScalar>`
//                        carries this; cost is read FROM the map's
//                        structure, never stored as a parallel field
//                        per algebra.dag's U1 no-engine discipline)
//   - occupancy_cost     per-kernel occupancy cost (registers-per-
//                        thread × threads-per-block, shared-memory-
//                        per-block) — the SIMT-specific cost
//                        contribution beyond per-instruction; how
//                        many warps the SM can co-schedule
//
// Both Int carriers are scaffold (same 🟡 bridge as Dim3): cost is a
// non-negative quantity, but without std/cardinality.dag refinement
// negative-cost is structurally representable. Producer-side
// invariant pending the cardinality substrate landing. PTX-ISA-
// version-specific cost tables (e.g. SASS instruction latencies per
// release) sit in the inhabitance walk that T-4's LanguageModel
// shape parameterizes, not in this carrier itself.
type PtxCost {
  instruction_cost: Int
  occupancy_cost: Int
}
